cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SMSD, please cite it as below, together with the article that developed the method you used (listed under references)."
type: software
title: "SMSD: Sequential Methods for Study Design"
authors:
  - family-names: Kelley
    given-names: Ken
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4756-8360"
    affiliation: "University of Notre Dame"
  - family-names: Darku
    given-names: "Francis Bilson"
  - family-names: Chattopadhyay
    given-names: Bhargab
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7713-3125"
version: 1.0.0
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/yelleKneK/SMSD"
abstract: >-
  Sequential methods for study design when the sample size cannot be fixed in
  advance because the parameters required to plan it are unknown. SMSD
  implements accuracy in parameter estimation (AIPE) and minimum risk point
  estimation (MRPE) procedures that collect data in stages and decide whether
  to keep sampling based on the data so far, along with a budget constrained
  sequential procedure for a linear contrast across K populations. It
  complements the MBESS and DMAR packages for fixed sample size analysis.
keywords:
  - R package
  - sequential analysis
  - sequential estimation
  - sample size planning
  - study design
  - accuracy in parameter estimation
  - minimum risk point estimation
  - effect sizes
  - confidence intervals
  - stopping rule
